\begin{didyouknow}{A String Inside a Matrix}
The matrix with $2$ on the diagonal and $-1$ just above and below is the finite-difference version of $-d^2/dx^2$. For $N$ grid points its eigenvalues are known exactly, $4\sin^2[k\pi/(2N+2)]$, and its eigenvectors are sampled sine waves $\sin(jk\pi/(N+1))$. These are the standing waves of a guitar string, so a simple matrix already contains the physics of vibrating strings.
\end{didyouknow}
% DYK-END

