\begin{beyond}
\textbf{Taken on trust: the Abel--Ruffini theorem.} There is no formula that expresses the roots of every polynomial of degree $5$ or higher in terms of its coefficients using only addition, subtraction, multiplication, division and $k$-th roots. The proof belongs to Galois theory and is found in standard texts on abstract algebra. Every polynomial with leading coefficient $1$ is, up to sign, the characteristic polynomial of some matrix (a so-called companion matrix), so no such formula can exist for the eigenvalues of general $n\times n$ matrices with $n\ge5$ either.
\end{beyond}

